<div id="thought-chart" class="thought-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparative chart">
<table class="thought-table">
<caption class="thought-sr-only">Major thought experiments, metaphors, and parables in Kongzi, Mozi, Mengzi, Laozi, Zhuangzi, Xunzi, and Han Feizi, with passage references.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Thinker</th><th scope="col">Image or experiment</th><th scope="col">What happens</th><th scope="col">What it helps argue</th><th scope="col">Where to find it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-roots-goodness" data-search="Youzi filial piety ren humaneness family root Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-roots-goodness">The roots of Goodness</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Master You compares respect for parents and elders to roots from which the moral Way grows.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Family relationships provide a starting point for cultivating humane conduct and social order.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 1.2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 5.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-pole-star" data-search="North Star Polaris government virtue de Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-pole-star">The Pole Star</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A ruler governing through virtue resembles the Pole Star, remaining in place while the other stars turn toward it.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">A ruler’s moral authority can organize others through example and attraction.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 2.1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 7.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-punishment-ritual" data-search="law regulations evasion shame conscience virtue self correction Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-punishment-ritual">Punishment or ritual</a><small class="thought-kind">Comparison</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Kongzi contrasts people evading punishments with people guided by virtue and ritual, who feel shame and correct themselves.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Government should cultivate an internal moral response rather than rely only on fear of penalties.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 2.3</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 7.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-lifelong-learning" data-search="Confucius fifteen thirty forty fifty sixty seventy learning cultivation">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-lifelong-learning">Learning from fifteen to seventy</a><small class="thought-kind">Self-portrait</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Kongzi describes stages of learning: at seventy, he can follow his desires without crossing the bounds of proper conduct.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Long cultivation can bring desire and proper conduct into agreement; moral maturity is more than reluctantly obeying rules.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 2.4</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 8.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-eight-dancers" data-search="Ji family royal dancers ceremony rank usurpation Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-eight-dancers">Eight rows of dancers</a><small class="thought-kind">Moral example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Kongzi sees eight rows of dancers in the Ji family’s courtyard and treats the display as evidence of deeper wrongdoing.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Ritual forms carry political limits: taking a superior’s ceremonial privileges challenges the order those rites express.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 3.1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 9.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-single-thread" data-search="Zengzi zhong shu loyalty reciprocity thread Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-single-thread">One thread through the teaching</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Kongzi says one thread joins his teachings. Master Zeng explains it as dutifulness tempered by sympathetic understanding.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">The many practices of moral life form a connected approach, requiring both commitment and sensitivity to others.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 4.15</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 13–14.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-gu-vessel" data-search="goblet cup ritual vessel rectification names gu Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-gu-vessel">A wine vessel that fails its name</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Kongzi asks whether a gu—a ritual wine vessel—that is not a proper gu can really deserve the name.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">A name carries standards: keeping a label is not enough when the thing or role fails to meet its requirements.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 6.25</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 20.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-helping-others-stand" data-search="near at hand standing oneself reciprocal golden rule shu sympathetic understanding Zigong Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-helping-others-stand">Helping others take their stand</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Asked about helping everyone, Kongzi turns to a nearer model: help others achieve what you seek for yourself, and do not impose what you dislike.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">One’s own aims and aversions offer a starting analogy for humane treatment of others.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · pp. 1–2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 6.30; 15.24</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 21, 45.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-four-corners" data-search="door mind tongue learning inference Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-four-corners">One corner and the other three</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A teacher shows one corner of a problem and expects the student to find the other three.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Learning requires active inference and a desire to understand; the teacher cannot do all the student’s thinking.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 7.8</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 22.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-walking-companions" data-search="three people walking teachers emulate self reflection Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-walking-companions">Two walking companions</a><small class="thought-kind">Moral example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Walking with two other people, Kongzi treats their good qualities as models and their faults as reminders to examine himself.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Ordinary encounters can teach moral improvement, including encounters with people whose conduct should not be copied.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 7.22</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 23.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-heavy-burden" data-search="Zengzi scholar official long way dao journey Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-heavy-burden">A heavy burden and a long road</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Master Zeng calls Goodness a heavy burden carried along a road that ends only at death.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral responsibility demands endurance and lifelong commitment, rather than a single achievement.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 8.7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 25.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-kuang" data-search="King Wen Heaven culture danger cultural transmission Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-kuang">Surrounded in Kuang</a><small class="thought-kind">Anecdote</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Surrounded in Kuang, Kongzi argues that Heaven would not entrust inherited culture to him only to let it perish there.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">His sense of responsibility for cultural transmission sustains confidence under threat.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 9.5</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 26.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-elusive-goal" data-search="Yan Yuan high hard before behind culture rites teacher Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-elusive-goal">Yan Hui’s receding goal</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Yan Hui describes the teaching as ever higher, harder, and just beyond reach, while Kongzi guides him through culture and ritual.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral learning combines sustained aspiration with gradual training; difficulty can deepen commitment rather than end it.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 9.11</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 26–27.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-one-day-rites" data-search="self restraint ren li eyes ears words movement Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-one-day-rites">One day of returning to ritual</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Kongzi says that one day of self-restraint and return to ritual could draw the world toward Goodness; he specifies looking, listening, speaking, and moving.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Goodness depends on one’s own disciplined practice, which Kongzi presents as capable of influencing the wider world.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 12.1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 33.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-food-weapons-trust" data-search="Zigong arms armaments confidence people faith government Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-food-weapons-trust">Food, weapons, and public trust</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Forced to surrender necessities of government, Kongzi gives up weapons first and food next, preserving the people’s confidence.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Public trust is indispensable to a state’s survival, even when compared with military strength and material provision.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 12.7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 34–35.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-roles-grain" data-search="Duke Jing Qi father son minister names duties Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-roles-grain">True lords and uneaten grain</a><small class="thought-kind">Moral example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Kongzi says lords, ministers, fathers, and sons must fulfill their roles. Duke Jing asks whether plentiful grain could feed him if they did not.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Resources alone cannot sustain political life when people fail to meet the responsibilities attached to their positions.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 12.11</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 36.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-wind-grass" data-search="Ji Kangzi executions killing gentleman petty person leadership Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-wind-grass">Wind and grass</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Asked about executing bad people, Kongzi compares a leader’s virtue to wind and the people’s response to grass bending before it.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">The ruler should improve his own conduct; moral example is presented as an alternative to governing through executions.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 12.19</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 36.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-wrong-names" data-search="rectification of names zhengming Zilu Wei penalties words Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-wrong-names">Wrong names, failing government</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Asked what he would do in office, Kongzi traces a failure to correct names through confused speech, failed tasks, broken ritual, and misdirected punishments.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Clear, fitting names and responsibilities are conditions for coordinated action and just government.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 13.3</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 37–38.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-father-sheep" data-search="Upright Gong Duke She sheep theft concealment father son loyalty corruption Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-father-sheep">The father who stole a sheep</a><small class="thought-kind">Moral dilemma</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A duke praises a son for reporting his father’s theft. Kongzi locates uprightness instead in fathers and sons protecting one another.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Family loyalty can conflict with public enforcement; the passage challenges the idea that reporting wrongdoing is always the upright act.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 13.18</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 39.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="kongzi" data-coverage="course" id="kongzi-mourning-rice" data-search="Zai Wo three years mourning brocade parents child care Confucius">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Kongzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#kongzi-mourning-rice">Sweet rice during mourning</a><small class="thought-kind">Moral dilemma</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Zai Wo wants shorter mourning and admits he could enjoy rich food and fine clothes. Kongzi asks whether he has forgotten his parents’ first three years of care.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mourning should express felt gratitude and grief, not merely compliance with an external rule.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/15t6ujvr7xGi_22mtf1Thf5dJVObqakMY/view">Kongzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Analects 17.21</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Edward Slingerland, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 49–50.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-archers-charioteers" data-search="worthy merit office state treasures archery chariots">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-archers-charioteers">Rewarding archers and charioteers</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A ruler encourages skilled archers and charioteers by rewarding and honoring them; Mozi proposes treating worthy officials in the same way.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Public rewards can encourage the abilities a state needs. Mozi calls worthy people its treasures and urges rulers to promote them.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 8</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 60.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-one-person-one-standard" data-search="state of nature birds beasts disagreement moral norms remonstrance">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-one-person-one-standard">One person, one standard; ten people, ten standards</a><small class="thought-kind">Origin story</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Mozi imagines a time before government when people condemn one another’s judgments, let surplus goods rot, and refuse to help. They choose a worthy ruler.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">He uses this disorder to argue for shared standards organized through a hierarchy. He also requires people to point out wrongdoing by superiors.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 11</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 62–63.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-flood-fire" data-search="constructive criticism replacement partiality">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-flood-fire">Adding water to a flood or flame to a fire</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Condemning someone’s proposal without offering a replacement is compared to adding water to a flood or flame to a fire.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Criticism must provide a constructive alternative; Mozi proposes impartial care in place of partiality.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 16</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 65.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-family-before-war" data-search="parents wife children guardian impartial care universal love">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-family-before-war">Entrusting one’s family before war</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Before leaving for a war one might not survive, one must choose an impartial or partial person to care for one’s family.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mozi argues that even critics of impartial care rely on it when their own family’s safety is at stake.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 16</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 67.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-epidemic-rulers" data-search="plague hunger cold impartial partial">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-epidemic-rulers">Choosing a ruler during an epidemic</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">An epidemic leaves people hungry, cold, and unburied. Would they choose a ruler who puts their needs first or one who neglects them?</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mozi argues that people’s choice in a crisis reveals the appeal of impartial government, even if they reject impartiality in words.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 16</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 68.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-orchard-theft-war" data-search="plums horses cattle spear sword murder conquest aggression">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-orchard-theft-war">From stolen peaches to aggressive war</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">People condemn stealing fruit, taking livestock, and killing someone for possessions, yet praise a state that attacks another state.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mozi challenges the double standard: harming others for one’s own benefit does not become right when a state does it on a larger scale.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 73.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-black-white" data-search="colors colours moral consistency scale">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-black-white">Calling a little black black, but much black white</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Someone correctly names a small amount of black but calls a large amount white.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mozi compares this inconsistency to condemning individual wrongdoing while praising aggressive war; greater scale does not reverse the moral judgment.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 73.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-useful-clothing" data-search="moderation expenditures luxury useful usefulness wealth">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-useful-clothing">Clothes that keep people warm and cool</a><small class="thought-kind">Practical example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Mozi asks what clothes are for: protection from winter cold and summer heat. Additions that do not serve that purpose should be removed.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Evaluate expenditure by the needs it meets. Reducing waste can improve a state’s resources without conquering more territory.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 20</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 74–75.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-rival-funeral-claims" data-search="three benefits Yao Shun Yu Tang Wen Wu mourning">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-rival-funeral-claims">Rival funeral customs claiming the same sages</a><small class="thought-kind">Policy test</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Two groups defend conflicting funeral practices while both claim to follow the ancient sage-kings. Mozi asks what their practices would actually accomplish.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Claims of ancient authority do not settle the dispute. Test funeral customs by their effects on wealth, population, and public order.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 25</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 77.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-harvest-without-plowing" data-search="ploughing lavish funerals treasury gold jade pearls coffins burial">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-harvest-without-plowing">Seeking a harvest while forbidding plowing</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Lavish tombs bury existing wealth, while prolonged mourning interrupts productive life. Mozi compares seeking wealth this way to demanding a harvest without allowing plowing.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Practices that consume resources and prevent their renewal undermine the prosperity they are supposed to support.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 25</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 78–79.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-modest-funeral" data-search="three layers clothes mound filial filiality sacrifice">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-modest-funeral">A modest coffin, tears, and a return to work</a><small class="thought-kind">Practical example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Mozi prescribes a modest coffin and grave, crying at burial, a prompt return to work, and continued offerings to one’s parents.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moderation allows grief and respect for parents while preserving livelihoods. His proposal does not abolish burial, mourning, or sacrifice.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 25</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 85.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-music-hunger" data-search="zithers luxury musical performances clothing rest">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-music-hunger">Pleasant music alongside hunger and exhaustion</a><small class="thought-kind">Comparison</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Mozi admits that bells, drums, and pipes sound pleasing, but says producing musical instruments keeps hungry people from food and weary workers from rest.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Enjoyment alone does not justify public expense. He judges musical performances by their costs to people’s basic needs.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 32</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 99.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-spinning-potters-wheel" data-search="three gauges precedent evidence application fatalism standards assessment">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-spinning-potters-wheel">Finding sunrise on a spinning potter’s wheel</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Trying to judge a claim without a stable standard is like marking sunrise and sunset on the surface of a spinning potter’s wheel.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Claims need reliable tests. Mozi names three: ancient precedents, what people have observed, and the benefits of putting a claim into practice.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 35</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 104.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mozi" data-coverage="course" id="mozi-searching-dead-parent" data-search="corpse mourning roof hypocrisy Confucians">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mozi-searching-dead-parent">Searching wells and rat holes for a dead parent</a><small class="thought-kind">Polemical example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Mozi depicts a Confucian mourner looking from a roof into a well and searching rat holes and wash basins for a deceased parent.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mozi attacks the search as foolish or insincere, presenting it as evidence against Confucian mourning practices. This is his hostile portrayal of his rivals.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1vN4xE5kwiAbW4zoQmdm5IKb6HRSL0jqe/view">Mozi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mozi chapter 39</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 105.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-fifty-paces">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-fifty-paces">Fifty paces laughing at a hundred</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Soldiers who flee fifty paces mock those who flee a hundred; both have run away.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">A king whose government is only less harmful than his neighbors’ has not yet achieved benevolent rule.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 1A3</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 122.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-blaming-weapon">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-blaming-weapon">Blaming the year or the weapon</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A ruler blames famine deaths on the bad year, like a killer blaming the weapon.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Rulers remain responsible for preventable suffering; pointing to external conditions does not excuse failed government.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 1A3</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Irene Bloom, trans., <i>Mencius</i> (2009), p. 4.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-animals-devour">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-animals-devour">Leading animals to devour people</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">The ruler’s animals are well fed while people starve; Mengzi compares death through government with death by a blade.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Lavish provision for the ruler alongside neglected human hunger makes government itself destructive.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · pp. 1, 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 1A4; 3B9</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Irene Bloom, trans., <i>Mencius</i> (2009), p. 4.</p><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 138.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-ox-sheep">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-ox-sheep">King, ox, and sheep</a><small class="thought-kind">Dialogue</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A king spares a frightened ox but substitutes a sheep he has not seen.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Compassion can be real yet selective; the king should extend it to his people.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 1A7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 123–124.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-mount-tai" data-search="feather wagon firewood strength eyesight ability">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-mount-tai">Lifting Mount Tai or helping an elder</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Carrying Mount Tai over the sea is impossible; massaging an elder’s stiff joints is not. The king fails to use capacities he has, like strength not used to lift a feather.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Failure to extend compassion is a failure to act, not proof that benevolent government is beyond the king’s ability.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 1A7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 124.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-trapping-people">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-trapping-people">Trapping the people</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A ruler leaves people without a secure livelihood, then punishes the crimes into which they fall.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Benevolent rule must make ordinary life sustainable; punishment alone ignores the conditions government creates.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · pp. 3–4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 1A7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 126.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-mere-fellow">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-mere-fellow">The tyrant as a mere fellow</a><small class="thought-kind">Dialogue</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Asked about killing a ruler, Mengzi describes the executed tyrant Zhou as someone who had already forfeited the standing of a ruler.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">A political title depends on fulfilling moral duties; tyranny can strip its holder of the protection that the title normally gives.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 5</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 1B8</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 128.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-seedlings">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-seedlings">Pulling up seedlings</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">An impatient farmer pulls his seedlings upward to help them grow; they wither.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral development needs patient cultivation: neither neglect growth nor force it.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 2A2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 130.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-child-well">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-child-well">Child at the well</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Someone suddenly sees a child about to fall into a well and feels alarm and concern before calculating personal advantage.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">People have a spontaneous beginning of compassion; the example does not guarantee that everyone will carry out a rescue.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 2A6</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 132–133.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-four-sprouts" data-search="four limbs fire spring">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-four-sprouts">Four sprouts</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Compassion, shame, deference, and moral judgment are young sprouts; developing them is like a fire catching or a spring breaking through.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">These beginnings of benevolence, righteousness, ritual propriety, and wisdom need cultivation to develop fully.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 2A6</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 132–133.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-parents-ditch">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-parents-ditch">Parents’ bodies abandoned in a ditch</a><small class="thought-kind">Imagined origin story</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">People see animals feeding on their unburied parents and visibly recoil, without trying to impress anyone.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Spontaneous feeling for parents helps ground burial practices; Mengzi challenges the Mohist account of impartial care.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 3A5</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 136.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-wide-house" data-search="great man integrity">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-wide-house">The wide house and great Way</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">The great person inhabits the world’s wide house and walks its great Way, remaining steadfast through wealth, poverty, and coercion.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral integrity, not the ability to frighten rulers or obtain office, defines greatness.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 5</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 3B2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Irene Bloom, trans., <i>Mencius</i> (2009), p. 62.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-drowning-sister">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-drowning-sister">Drowning sister-in-law; drowning world</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">One must grasp a drowning sister-in-law despite a rule against touching. Saving a disordered world, however, requires the Way rather than a literal helping hand.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral judgment can require departing from a ritual rule; extending an analogy also requires attention to the different case.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 4A17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 140–141.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-heaven-people">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-heaven-people">Heaven’s eyes and ears</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Heaven does not announce a successor: Shun’s conduct, orderly administration, and the people’s acceptance reveal its judgment.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Political legitimacy requires more than a ruler’s private gift of the realm; Heaven’s judgment is manifested through the people.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 5</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 5A5</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Irene Bloom, trans., <i>Mencius</i> (2009), pp. 103–104.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-willow-cups">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-willow-cups">Willow tree made into cups and bowls</a><small class="thought-kind">Disputed analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Gaozi compares moral formation to carving willow wood; Mengzi objects that carving violates the tree.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mengzi rejects a model that makes goodness an injury imposed on human nature from outside.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · pp. 1–2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 6A1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 146.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-water">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-water">Water flowing downward</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Water naturally flows downward, although an external force can drive it upward.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Human nature tends toward goodness; circumstances can divert that tendency.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 6A2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 146.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-ox-mountain">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-ox-mountain">Ox Mountain</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Logging and grazing strip a once-wooded mountain until it appears naturally barren.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Damaged moral capacities do not show that human nature was originally bad; nourishment and conditions matter.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 6A8</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 150–151.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-fish-bear-paw">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-fish-bear-paw">Fish and bear’s paw</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">When two desired foods cannot both be had, one chooses the preferred one; Mengzi compares this with choosing righteousness over life.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">People can rank desires: preserving life is not necessarily their highest commitment.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 6A10</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 151.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-contemptuous-food">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-contemptuous-food">Food given with contempt</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A hungry person may refuse food offered insultingly, yet someone accepts a lavish salary without checking whether it is right.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral shame can outweigh survival; wealth can nevertheless lead people to abandon the judgment they once upheld.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 6A10</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 151–152.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="mengzi" data-coverage="course" id="mengzi-child-parents">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Mengzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#mengzi-child-parents">A child’s love for parents</a><small class="thought-kind">Example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Young children love their parents without studying; older children recognize respect for elder siblings.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Mengzi finds beginnings of benevolence and righteousness in ordinary relationships and calls for extending them more widely.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1HcSH79w3GG_9rvU5PoWuBZFUAS7aPPYb/view">Mengzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Mengzi 7A15</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Bryan W. Van Norden, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 153.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-filled-bellies">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-filled-bellies">Filled bellies and strong bones</a><small class="thought-kind">Political image</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Sage rulers fill bellies and strengthen bones while weakening ambitions and reducing knowledge and desires.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Order depends on meeting basic needs and curbing competitive desires; the ruler actively shapes the people.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 3</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 174.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-water">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-water">Water</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Water nourishes everything, occupies low places, and overcomes what is hard despite its softness.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Humility, non-contention, and flexibility can be more effective than force.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 8, 78</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 176; pp. 208–209.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-empty-spaces">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-empty-spaces">Empty hub, vessel, and room</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Wheels, pots, and rooms work because of their empty spaces.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Absence and openness make useful activity possible; solidity alone is insufficient.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 11</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 177.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-unnoticed-ruler">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-unnoticed-ruler">The scarcely noticed ruler</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Rulers range from barely noticed to loved, feared, and despised. Under the best, people regard their order as simply how things are.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Effective leadership need not advertise itself or make people feel dependent on a ruler.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 180.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="further" id="laozi-uncarved-block">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-uncarved-block">Uncarved block</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Unworked wood represents simplicity before carving divides it into specialized objects.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Preserve simplicity; avoid excessive desires and imposed distinctions.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><strong class="thought-outside">Outside the course packet</strong><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://scholarworks.iu.edu/dspace/items/d79d75bd-6464-4b8f-9fe8-efa076c36ea2">Robert Eno translation</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 28, 32</span></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-world-vessel">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-world-vessel">The world as a spiritual vessel</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">The world is likened to a sacred vessel: those who try to manipulate or grasp it ruin or lose it.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">The world cannot safely be treated as an object for a ruler’s controlling purposes.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 29</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 186.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-war-mourning">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-war-mourning">Weeds after war; victory as mourning</a><small class="thought-kind">Political image</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">An army leaves thorns and failed harvests; even victory calls for mourning.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Military force creates lasting damage; it should be a last resort, and killing should never be celebrated.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 30–31</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 186–187.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-ritual-sleeves">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-ritual-sleeves">The ritualist rolls up his sleeves</a><small class="thought-kind">Vignette</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">When others fail to respond, the person insisting on ritual correctness rolls up his sleeves and resorts to force.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Enforced ritual can reveal depleted trust and produce coercion instead of harmony.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 38</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 190.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-court-granaries">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-court-granaries">The rich court and empty granaries</a><small class="thought-kind">Political image</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Courts glitter while fields grow weeds and granaries stand empty; rulers consume too much in taxes while the people go hungry.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Elite consumption can impoverish the people; luxury amid deprivation resembles robbery, not good government.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · pp. 3–4</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 53, 75</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 197, 207.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="further" id="laozi-infant">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-infant">Infant</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A baby is physically soft yet holds firmly and embodies vitality and harmony.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Genuine strength can coexist with softness and freedom from calculated striving.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><strong class="thought-outside">Outside the course packet</strong><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://ctext.org/dao-de-jing/ens">James Legge translation</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 55</span></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-small-fish">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-small-fish">Cooking small fish</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Governing a large state is compared to cooking delicate fish.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">A common reading: restrained government avoids damaging what it seeks to nourish through excessive handling.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 60</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 200.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-sea-valleys">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-sea-valleys">The sea below the valleys</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Rivers and the sea receive the valleys’ waters because they occupy the lower position.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Leaders gain authority by placing themselves below and behind the people, rather than competing with them.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 66</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 204.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-soft-living">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-soft-living">Soft bodies, stiff corpses, breaking trees</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Living bodies are soft; corpses are rigid. An overly rigid tree breaks.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Flexibility belongs to life; apparent hardness can signal fragility and decline.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 76</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 207–208.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="laozi" data-coverage="course" id="laozi-small-community">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Laozi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#laozi-small-community">Small communities that never visit</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A small population leaves labor-saving tools unused; neighbors hear one another’s animals but never visit.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">An ideal of local contentment challenges expansion, technological ambition, and restless travel.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1OQQA2ZIZEfZrHWueWSEc8zKFTQfzJLU1/view">Laozi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Daodejing 80</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Philip J. Ivanhoe, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 209–210.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-useless-tree">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-useless-tree">Useless tree</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A nimble weasel dies in a trap; a crooked tree escapes the carpenter and offers a place to rest.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Conventional usefulness can endanger life; apparent uselessness can protect freedom.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 229.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-giant-bird" data-search="Kun Peng Minnow Breeze cicada dove quail bird">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-giant-bird">Giant bird and small creatures</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A giant fish, Kun (Minnow), becomes Peng (Breeze). Small flyers mock its journey; short-lived mushrooms and insects cannot know longer cycles.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Limited experience can make other lives seem absurd; scale and lifespan shape what a creature can understand.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 224–225.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-giant-gourds">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-giant-gourds">Giant gourds</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Huizi smashes enormous gourds that fail as containers and ladles; Zhuangzi proposes using them as floats.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Usefulness depends on imagination and context; familiar purposes can conceal new possibilities.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 228–229.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-butterfly-dream">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-butterfly-dream">Butterfly dream</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Zhou dreams he is a butterfly; awake, he wonders whether he is a butterfly dreaming he is Zhou.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Questions certainty about identity and perspective; evokes the transformation of things.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 238.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-pipes-wind">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-pipes-wind">Wind and the ten thousand pipes</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">One wind blows through differently shaped hollows, producing many distinct sounds.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Different forms yield different voices; the image invites reflection on how perspectives arise without a single imposed voice.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 229–230.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-species-standards">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-species-standards">Whose home, taste, or beauty?</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Humans, eels, and monkeys prefer different homes; animals eat different foods and flee women celebrated as beautiful.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Human standards are not automatically universal; judgments arise from particular forms of life.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 236.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-neutral-judge">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-neutral-judge">Who can judge the dispute?</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A proposed judge already agrees or disagrees with the disputants. Choosing another person does not by itself settle who is right.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Winning an argument or appointing a judge cannot simply be assumed to establish an independent standard of truth.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 2</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 238.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-cook-ding">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-cook-ding">Cook Ding</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">His knife follows the spaces within an ox’s joints, remaining sharp for years. At difficult joints, he slows down.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Practiced responsiveness to a situation’s structure preserves both skill and life.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 3</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 239–240.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-hundun">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-hundun">Hundun / All-full (Chaos)</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Two rulers repay Hundun’s kindness by boring seven human openings into him; he dies.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Imposing supposedly beneficial standards can destroy another being’s way of life.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 256.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-mirror-heart">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-mirror-heart">Heart-mind as a mirror</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">The perfected person’s heart-mind responds like a mirror, reflecting what comes without holding on to it.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Receptive, adaptable action avoids clinging to preconceptions and possessions; emptiness need not mean inactivity.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 256.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-happy-fish">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-happy-fish">Happiness of fish</a><small class="thought-kind">Dialogue</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Huizi challenges Zhuangzi’s claim to know that swimming fish are happy.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Explores how we know other beings’ experiences and the limits of argumentative certainty.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 260–261.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-turtle">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-turtle">Turtle in the mud</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Offered office, Zhuangzi asks whether a turtle would prefer honored bones or life in the mud.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Living freely can be worth more than prestigious political service.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 260.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-frog-well">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-frog-well">Frog in the collapsed well</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A frog boasts about its well until a sea turtle, too large to enter, describes the ocean.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">A familiar world can feel complete until a wider perspective exposes its limits.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 259–260.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-wife-tub">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-wife-tub">Singing after his wife’s death</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">After first grieving, Zhuangzi beats a tub and sings, understanding his wife’s death as another transformation like the seasons.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Seeing death within continuing transformation can alter mourning; the story explicitly acknowledges his initial grief.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 18</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 261.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="zhuangzi" data-coverage="course" id="zhuangzi-predator-chain">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Zhuangzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#zhuangzi-predator-chain">Cicada, mantis, magpie, and hunter</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Zhuangzi pursues a magpie hunting a mantis stalking a cicada—then is chased by the grove’s warden.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Fixation on an attractive prize can hide one’s own vulnerability; even Zhuangzi can become entangled.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1l-Jgd-W5RVkhGuOjMN8NmjuGqh2j_1w0/view">Zhuangzi packet · pp. 2–3</a><span class="thought-passage">Zhuangzi 20</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Paul Kjellberg, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 263.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-crooked-wood" data-search="crooked wood dull metal shaping frame steaming bending wheel">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-crooked-wood">Shaping wood and sharpening metal</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Crooked wood is straightened, a straight beam bent into a lasting wheel, and dull metal sharpened.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Teaching, ritual, and deliberate practice can produce lasting changes in dispositions; improvement need not leave the original material unchanged.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · pp. 1, 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 1, 23</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 270–271, 310.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-blue-dye">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-blue-dye">Blue dye and ice</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Dye exceeds its plant’s color; ice is colder than water.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Learning can transform people beyond their original condition.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 270.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-small-steps">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-small-steps">Small steps and accumulating streams</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Earth accumulates into a mountain and water into a deep pool; long journeys and rivers depend on small steps and streams.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral achievement comes through accumulated, sustained practice.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 271–272.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-crying-children">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-crying-children">Same infant cries, different customs</a><small class="thought-kind">Example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Children begin with the same cries but grow up with different customs through teaching.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Education and social surroundings shape conduct; acquired differences should not simply be treated as inborn.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 270–271.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-chariot-boat">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-chariot-boat">Chariot and horses; boat and oars</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A rider travels far without better feet; a passenger crosses water without becoming a swimmer.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Learning includes using external resources and supports well; cultivated ability need not come from exceptional birth.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 271.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-nags-carving">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-nags-carving">Old nags and persistent carving</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A fine horse’s single leap is limited; a nag’s continued travel and carving that cuts metal and stone show what persistence achieves.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Persistence and accumulated effort can achieve what a brief display of talent cannot.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 1</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 271–272.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-oxen-horses">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-oxen-horses">Humans commanding oxen and horses</a><small class="thought-kind">Comparison</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Humans are weaker than oxen and slower than horses, yet put both to use.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Cooperation organized through shared standards, roles, and divisions gives human communities their strength.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 9</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 281.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-boat-water">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-boat-water">Ruler as boat, people as water</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Water carries a boat but can also overturn it; Xunzi applies the received saying to rulers and common people.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">The ruler’s security depends on governing fairly and caring for the people whose support sustains the state.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · pp. 1–2</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 9</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., <i>Xunzi: The Complete Text</i> (2014), p. 70.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-human-omens">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-human-omens">Falling stars and human ill omens</a><small class="thought-kind">Comparison</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">People fear falling stars and groaning trees, while neglected fields and disordered government bring famine.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">The dangers to address arise from human mismanagement, not a supposed moral message in unusual natural events.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 285.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-rain-sacrifice">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-rain-sacrifice">Rain after a rain sacrifice</a><small class="thought-kind">Causal test</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Rain falls after a sacrifice, but it also falls without one. Xunzi retains the ritual while rejecting the proposed causal explanation.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Ritual gives human affairs proper form; its value need not depend on controlling the weather.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 17</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 286.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-potter">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-potter">Potter and clay</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A potter deliberately makes a vessel from clay.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Sages create ritual and moral standards through cultivated activity, rather than receiving them as spontaneous products of inborn nature.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 23</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 312.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="xunzi" data-coverage="course" id="xunzi-walking-world">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Xunzi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#xunzi-walking-world">Feet that could walk the world</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A person has feet capable of walking anywhere, although nobody actually walks everywhere.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">The capacity to become a sage is distinct from realizing it: universal potential does not make achievement automatic.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LcgBOfcWDZgdmHDnDAIw9LMDbMGlualJ/view">Xunzi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Xunzi 23</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eric L. Hutton, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 317.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-ministers-polishing">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-ministers-polishing">Ministers carving and polishing themselves</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">When a ruler reveals his desires, ministers reshape their presentation to match what he wants.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Keeping preferences hidden makes flattery harder and helps the ruler assess ministers by their work.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 5</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 324–325.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-ink-line">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-ink-line">The ink line and crooked wood</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A carpenter’s marking line stays straight instead of bending around crooked wood.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Legal standards should not bend for noble status: powerful ministers face punishment, and commoners can earn rewards.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 6</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 332–333.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-two-handles">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-two-handles">Two handles; tiger’s claws and teeth</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A tiger surrendering its weapons to a dog becomes vulnerable.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">The ruler must retain control over rewards and punishments.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 333.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-cap-robe">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-cap-robe">Cap keeper and robe keeper</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">The cap keeper covers a sleeping ruler; both officials are punished.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Officials must fulfill defined responsibilities without neglect or unauthorized interference.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 1</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 7</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 335.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-fame-profit">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-fame-profit">Persuading someone who wants fame—or profit</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">An adviser tries promises of reputation or wealth on rulers with different public and private motives.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Persuasion depends on understanding the hearer’s aims and self-image, not simply on knowing the facts or arguing clearly.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 12</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 336–338.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-half-eaten-peach">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-half-eaten-peach">Mi Zixia: borrowed carriage and half-eaten peach</a><small class="thought-kind">Story</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A ruler first praises Mi Zixia’s unauthorized carriage ride and shared peach, then condemns the same acts after affection fades.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Personal favor can reverse judgments of unchanged conduct; advisers cannot assume that correctness protects them from a ruler’s changing feelings.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 12</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 339–340.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-inverted-scales" data-search="reverse reversed scales">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-inverted-scales">The dragon’s inverted scales</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A dragon can be ridden, but touching the reversed scales beneath its throat provokes a deadly response.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Persuaders must recognize a ruler’s dangerous sensitivities; access and familiarity do not guarantee safety.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 2</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 12</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 340.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="further" id="han-feizi-carriages-coffins">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-carriages-coffins">Carriage makers and coffin makers</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">One hopes customers prosper; the other benefits when people die.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Incentives shape conduct; apparent kindness or cruelty may reflect material interests.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><strong class="thought-outside">Outside the course packet</strong><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://cognitionandculture.net/wp-content/uploads/1939The-Complete-Works-of-Han-Fei-Tzu-A-Classic-of-Chinese-Political-Science-ebook.pdf#page=86">W. K. Liao translation · PDF p. 86</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 17</span></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-landowner-workers">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-landowner-workers">The landowner and hired field workers</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A landowner offers good food and pay to secure diligent labor; workers labor hard to obtain those benefits.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Aligned interests can sustain cooperation without affection, while frustrated expectations can produce resentment even between parents and children.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 32</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Christoph Harbsmeier, trans., <i>Han Feizi: The Art of Statecraft in Early China</i> (2025), vol. 2, pp. 562–563; hf 32.3.1.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-spear-shield">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-spear-shield">Spear and shield</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A seller claims his shield stops everything and his spear pierces everything, but cannot say what happens when they meet.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Claims of unstoppable moral worth and positional power that stops everything cannot both hold; the story exposes a contradiction in the chapter’s political debate.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · pp. 3–4</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 40</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 351–352.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-dragon-clouds">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-dragon-clouds">Dragons on clouds; snakes on mist</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A flying dragon and winged snake lose their extraordinary powers when the clouds and mist supporting them disperse.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Shen Dao, quoted here, uses this image to explain dependence on positional power; the chapter debates how far that claim can go.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 40</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 348.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-yao-jie">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-yao-jie">Yao without office; Jie on the throne</a><small class="thought-kind">Comparison</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">The virtuous Yao as a commoner cannot command even a few people; the tyrant Jie can disrupt the world as ruler.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Moral worth and power to command differ. Han Feizi’s reply advocates law-supported authority for ordinary rulers rather than dependence on exceptional sages.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · pp. 3–4</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 40</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 348, 351–352.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-sons-daughters">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-sons-daughters">Sons, daughters, and parental calculation</a><small class="thought-kind">Example</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Han Feizi describes parents celebrating a son’s birth but killing a daughter, explaining the contrast through expected future benefit.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">He argues that even family relations can involve calculations of advantage, challenging reliance on affection to secure political obedience.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 46</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Christoph Harbsmeier, trans., <i>Han Feizi: The Art of Statecraft in Early China</i> (2025), vol. 2, pp. 897–898; hf 46.3.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-mother-teacher-doctor">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-mother-teacher-doctor">The loving mother, teacher, and doctor</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A loving mother sends a misbehaving child to a teacher and a seriously ill child to a doctor.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Love alone does not supply the discipline or expertise needed to protect a child; Han Feizi questions making affection the basis of government.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 3</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 47</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 356.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-rabbit-stump">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-rabbit-stump">Rabbit and tree stump</a><small class="thought-kind">Parable</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A farmer abandons farming after one rabbit accidentally dies against a tree.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Policies must fit present conditions; copying ancient successes mechanically is foolish.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 49</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 358.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-nests-fire">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-nests-fire">Nests and fire drills in the wrong age</a><small class="thought-kind">Historical analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Sages invent shelters or ways to make fire for urgent needs; repeating those solutions in later ages would invite ridicule.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Successful methods answer particular conditions. Their ancient prestige does not make them suitable for every later problem.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 49</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 357.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-five-sons">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-five-sons">Five sons become twenty-five grandsons</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Five sons each have five sons; population expands while available goods become scarce.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Han Feizi explains growing conflict through changing material conditions, challenging the assumption that ancient peace proves superior virtue.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 4</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 49</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 358.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-five-vermin">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-five-vermin">Five vermin of the state</a><small class="thought-kind">Metaphor</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Scholars, persuaders, private swordsmen, those evading battle, and merchants/artisans are compared to pests consuming the state.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">He condemns activities he presents as diverting effort or authority from law, farming, and warfare—a political judgment, not a neutral description of these groups.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · pp. 4–5</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 49</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 371.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-straight-arrows">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-straight-arrows">Straight arrows and round wheels</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Craftspeople straighten arrow shafts and shape wheels instead of waiting for naturally perfect wood.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Reliable laws, rewards, and punishments govern ordinary people; a state cannot depend on rare, spontaneous goodness.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 5</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 50</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 376–377.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-child-boil" data-search="popping a child&#x27;s boil abscess opening">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-child-boil">Lancing a child’s boil</a><small class="thought-kind">Analogy</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">A child is held still while its caring mother lances a boil. It cries at the pain, unable to understand the benefit.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Han Feizi compares the people to the child: their resistance does not, he argues, disprove the benefit of harsh laws, taxes, or military service.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · pp. 5–6</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 50</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 378.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-absent-founders">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-absent-founders">Dead founders and rival heirs</a><small class="thought-kind">Thought experiment</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Eight Confucian branches and three Mohist branches disagree, yet each claims authenticity; their dead founders cannot settle the dispute.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Claims of ancient authority need evidence. Invoking a founder does not resolve disagreement over what that founder actually taught.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 5</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 50</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), pp. 371–372.</p></details></td>
</tr>
<tr class="thought-row" data-thinker="han-feizi" data-coverage="course" id="han-feizi-competing-funerals">
<td data-label="Thinker"><strong>Han Feizi</strong></td>
<th scope="row" data-label="Image or experiment"><a class="thought-permalink" href="#han-feizi-competing-funerals">Frugal funeral or filial funeral?</a><small class="thought-kind">Comparison</small></th>
<td data-label="What happens">Rulers honor brief, inexpensive Mohist mourning as frugal and prolonged, costly Confucian mourning as filial.</td>
<td data-label="What it helps argue">Han Feizi challenges rulers who praise incompatible standards without deciding which conduct they will actually reward.</td>
<td class="thought-source" data-label="Where to find it"><a class="thought-source-link" href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1jGRtjrEVOKVYaWWDIvEGGwEBOari86GY/view">Han Feizi packet · p. 5</a><span class="thought-passage">Han Feizi 50</span><details class="thought-edition"><summary>Original edition</summary><p>Eirik Lang Harris, trans., in <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (2023), p. 372.</p></details></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
