<footer class="thought-notes">
<section id="thought-sources" class="thought-sources" aria-labelledby="thought-sources-heading">
<h2 id="thought-sources-heading">Sources and page numbers</h2>
<p><strong>114 examples come from the seven course packets linked in the chart.</strong> The three marked “Outside the course packet” are the uncarved block, the infant, and the carriage/coffin makers; their links open the named translations.</p>
<p><strong>Use the packet’s own page number.</strong> “Han Feizi packet · p. 5” means page 5 of that standalone PDF, numbered at the bottom. It is not page 5 of a combined daily reader. “Original edition” gives the translator, book, and book page used to prepare that excerpt.</p>
<p><strong>The chart’s descriptions and explanations are study paraphrases, not quotations.</strong> Read the linked passage for the author’s wording and context. “What it helps argue” offers an interpretation, not a line from the text.</p>
<details class="thought-bibliography">
<summary>Books used in the course packets</summary>
<ul>
<li><strong>Main anthology:</strong> Philip J. Ivanhoe and Bryan W. Van Norden, eds., <i>Readings in Classical Chinese Philosophy</i>, 3rd ed. (Hackett, 2023). Translators: Edward Slingerland (Kongzi), Philip J. Ivanhoe (Mozi), Bryan W. Van Norden (Mengzi), Philip J. Ivanhoe (Laozi), Paul Kjellberg (Zhuangzi), Eric L. Hutton (Xunzi), and Eirik Lang Harris (Han Feizi).</li>
<li><strong>Mengzi additions:</strong> Irene Bloom, trans., <i>Mencius</i> (Columbia University Press, 2009).</li>
<li><strong>Xunzi’s boat and water:</strong> Eric L. Hutton, trans., <i>Xunzi: The Complete Text</i> (Princeton University Press, 2014), p. 70.</li>
<li><strong>Han Feizi’s landowner/workers and sons/daughters:</strong> Christoph Harbsmeier, trans., <i>Han Feizi: The Art of Statecraft in Early China</i>, vol. 2 (Brill, 2025), pp. 562–563 and 897–898.</li>
</ul>
</details>
<details class="thought-reading-notes">
<summary>Reading notes</summary>
<p>Kongzi’s entries come from the <i>Analects</i>. Some passages record his disciples speaking; the chart identifies those speakers. Passage numbers follow the packet’s Edward Slingerland translation and may differ in other editions.</p>
<p>Names vary by translation: Kjellberg’s Minnow and Breeze are Kun and Peng; his All-full is Hundun. The chart includes thought experiments, metaphors, parables, and concrete comparisons; these categories overlap.</p>
</details>
</section>
</footer>
